 bounds we can prove that  no such polynomial  exists and for the lower bounds we  actually  prove something a bit stronger that even  the first three conditions here cannot  be  achieved ignoring the one in in red  and so for example it's now known that  for the planet clique problem  low degree algorithms succeed in the  conjectured  uh well in the easy regime where we know  that algorithms  succeed and they fail in the conjectured  hard regime  and this is not specific to planted  clique  actually for all sorts of different  problems  we now sort of believe that  polynomials of degree order log n  are as powerful as all known polynomial  time algorithms  so this has now been observed to be true  in  um planet cliques sparse bca etc  all these problems i listed at the  beginning  and so maybe we wonder you know why  so one explanation is that  well for these testing problems often  the best known algorithm takes the form  of some kind of spectral method  meaning you threshold the leading  eigenvalue of some matrix that you build  based on your  input and  using power iteration you can typically  implement such a  algorithm as a logarithmic degree  polynomial  and then conversely well it's not true  that  every logarithmic degree polynomial can  actually be evaluated in polynomial time  uh it turns out that for these uh  examples that we know of whenever there  is a log degree polynomial that succeeds  there is also uh you know a spectral  method or some kind of  polynomial time algorithm that works  and so this is quite nice it lets us uh  predict  uh fairly easily when various problems  should be easy  when they should be hard and give some  kind of concrete evidence for  um what makes the hard regime hard  and this is uh you know somewhat more  straightforward than proving say a sum  of squares lower bounds or an average  case reduction  and as a result this method is  applicable to a somewhat  broader class of problems  okay but i've written here that this  con