out the  symmetry  now in order to actually refute the  conjecture  we need to make the distributions  symmetric somehow  so we'd now like to construct  symmetric distributions q and p  meaning they're they're unchanged by any  permutation of the coordinates  and these are now going to be  distributions over r to the n  and we want sort of the same properties  q is just going to be iid  id gaussian  and we no low degree algorithm can  distinguish  q from p but some polynomial time  algorithm can distinguish q from  noisy version of p  um okay so basically we use the same  construction as before but now we need  to do something to make the  distributions  symmetric so here's the main idea  so instead of observing just a bit  string  we are now going to imagine observing  a list of these pairs i comma x sub i  so we're kind of like giving you the  bits in some random order  but each one is sort of tagged by its  position in the original string  and then this whole pair i comma x i is  going to be encoded by a single real  number as just a single  entry of our distribution  and then uh there's a few technical  things to deal with here of course there  could be  some conflicting information if the same  high gets chosen  twice and we'll actually just throw that  out  and so i won't show you the full details  of this  but essentially the same argument goes  through but now the error correcting  code now needs to handle  erasures as well as errors  so erasure is meaning that some bits  simply were not observed at all  so this occurs either when this  conflicting information is thrown out  or if by chance some i values simply do  not get  sampled by this random procedure you  do not observe all of the bits  um all right so that's basically the  idea of the proof  let me just summarize a bit to wrap up  so the goal here was to try to formalize  the class of problems for which we  believe  low degree polynomials are optimal among  all polynomial time algorithms  and so we ma